### [P3] Errored streaming turn is logged as "completed", polluting the #161 baseline
`src/agentg/runtime.py:495-496`

`_finish_turn`'s finally always calls `turn.finish()`, even when `_stream_text` raised mid-generation. The blocking path suppresses the log on error (via `TurnContext.__exit__` seeing `exc_type`), but a failed streaming turn still emits "turn completed ÔÇª", adding failed-turn durations to the latency baseline #161 exists to keep clean. Consider passing the error state to `finish()` and skipping the "completed" line on failure.

### [P3] Streaming #161 duration now includes Telegram delivery latency
`src/agentg/runtime.py:487-496` + `instrument.py:finish`

Because `finish()` fires only after the channel finishes consuming/editing messages, the streaming turn's wall-clock now folds in typing refreshes, per-sentence `edit_text` round-trips and rate-limit waits ÔÇö not just model latency. That's defensible as "wall-clock," but it conflates model and delivery latency in the same metric the issue calls a "latency baseline." Worth a note in the log line or the issue.

